<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Protest and freedom · Lament · 1798 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">The Wearing of the Green</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An Irish street song about the rising of 1798, when people who wore green, the rebels' colour, were punished harshly.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous; best-known version by Dion Boucicault</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional Irish air (first published 1841)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Irish</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Ireland</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>from 1798; Boucicault's version 1864</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Protest and freedom · Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous street ballad (from 1798); best-known version by Dion Boucicault, published in his play of 1864)</dd></div></dl>
<p class="kd-note">This song is about hard things from the past, like war, cruelty or death. You can read about it with a grown-up.</p><button type="button" class="kd-story sg-kd-open">Read about this song</button>
<div class="sg-body sg-fold">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-wearing-of-the-green">The Wearing of the Green</a>&quot; is an Irish street ballad lamenting the repression of the supporters of the Irish Rebellion of 1798. The revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-of-united-irishmen">Society of United Irishmen</a> had taken green as its colour, and its supporters wore green garments, ribbons or cockades; in some versions the green is shamrock rather than cloth. The song proclaims that men and women are being hanged for wearing it.</p><p>Many versions exist, sung to an old Irish air. In the usual form the narrator is a rebel who has gone into exile and meets a public figure, most often Napper Tandy, who asks for news of Ireland and is told that those who wear green are persecuted. Henry Halliday Sparling's Irish Minstrelsy (1888) prints the anonymous &quot;Green upon the Cape&quot;, dated to 1798, a longer poem in which a croppy from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belfast">Belfast</a> travels into exile and is questioned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> by &quot;Boney&quot;, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>. In &quot;Green on my Cape&quot;, published in Dundalk in 1802, it is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-emmet">Robert Emmet</a> who meets the narrator, at Brest, and versions of the 1840s and 1850s bring in Napoleon. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-grattan">Henry Grattan</a> Curran (1800–76), son of John Philpot Curran, wrote a version of his own and claimed that the original came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-tipperary">County Tipperary</a>.</p><p>The best-known words are Dion Boucicault's, written for his play Arragh na Pogue, or the Wicklow Wedding (1864), set in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-wicklow">County Wicklow</a> during the rebellion. Boucicault said he had based them on a half-remembered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a> street ballad. His second verse tells of the singer's meeting with Napper Tandy, the rebel leader exiled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and the third and last verses he added, in contrast to the defiance of the middle verse, advise emigration to America; Boucicault himself fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> after leaving his wife for a young actress. The tune was first printed in The Citizen, or Dublin Monthly Magazine in 1841, and a variant appeared as &quot;Up! For the Green&quot; in James Duffy's The Spirit of the Nation (Dublin, 1845); others are in Alfred Moffat's Minstrelsy of Ireland (1897) and Francis O'Neill's Music of Ireland (Chicago, 1903).</p><p>The air carried other words. &quot;The Rising of the Moon&quot; tells of the same rising of 1798, &quot;The Orange and the Green&quot; of a marriage between Protestant and Catholic, and in 1865, at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a>, &quot;The Wearing of the Grey&quot; lamented the Confederate army, while &quot;The Army of the Free&quot; was published to the same tune. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wellington">Wellington</a> Guernsey made a version for voice and piano in 1866, Charles Villiers Stanford set new words by Alfred Perceval Graves in 1900, and Joseph <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-ceallaigh">O'Kelly</a> wrote piano variations on it, his Air irlandais of 1877. Gerald O'Hara sings it in Margaret Mitchell's Gone with the Wind, and John McCormack (1904 and 1912) and Judy Garland (1940) recorded it; in 1962 Allan Sherman turned it into the comic &quot;Shake Hands With Your Uncle Max&quot;.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>An exile asks for news of Ireland and hears that those who wear the green of the rebels of 1798 are persecuted, and that men and women are hanged for it. A lament for a crushed rising and a song of defiance, it is mournful and bitter; in Boucicault's version it ends by turning to emigration across the sea.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><p class="sg-nowords sg-nowords-g" data-say>The words of this song are in the public domain, but no free, reliable edition has been found yet to import them from, and the Institute never types a song&#8217;s words from memory: Genius shows them below, under its own licence.</p><div class="sg-genius"><div class="sg-genius-btns"><button type="button" class="sg-genius-btn" data-genius="2051845" data-what="Lyrics">Show the lyrics from Genius</button></div><div class="sg-genius-frames"></div><p class="sg-genius-note">Genius shows the lyrics in its own frame, under its own licence: nothing loads from Genius until you press a button. <a href="https://genius.com/The-wolfe-tones-the-wearing-of-the-green-lyrics" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The song on Genius</a>.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="kY61CUOkwmk" data-title="The Wearing of the Green: John McCormack, tenor, recorded 1921"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">John McCormack, tenor, recorded 1921</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel John McCormack - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kY61CUOkwmk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Rising of the Moon</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same air</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Orange and the Green</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same air</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sae Will We Yet (The Corries)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same air</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Wearing of the Grey</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1865</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same air, a lament for the Confederate army</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Army of the Free</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1865</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same air</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Wearing of the Green (Wellington Guernsey)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1866</span><span class="sg-chain-h">version for voice and piano</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Wearing of the Green (Charles Villiers Stanford, words by Alfred Perceval Graves)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1900</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words to the melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Air irlandais, Op. 58 (Joseph O'Kelly)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1877</span><span class="sg-chain-h">piano variations on the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gone with the Wind (Margaret Mitchell)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1936</span><span class="sg-chain-h">sung in a novel</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Namgay Doolyat (Rudyard Kipling, in Life's Handicap)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">something like its words sung in a short story</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Shake Hands With Your Uncle Max (Allan Sherman)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1962</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody to the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Monto</span><span class="sg-chain-h">refers to it</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Each Dollar a Bullet (Stiff Little Fingers)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">refers to it</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Green upon the Cape</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1798</span><span class="sg-chain-h">earlier anonymous version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Green on my Cape</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1802</span><span class="sg-chain-h">earlier version, published in Dundalk</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">a Dublin street ballad</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Boucicault said he based his words on one he half remembered</span></li></ul></section>
